Case B: P1.3 serialization reading adjusted bounds — comment explicitly states serialization reads the ADJUSTED bounds and that's the intended contract ("序列化阶段读取的是调整后的 bounds"). So that's the design, not pollution.

Case C: absolutePosition computed in construct happens BEFORE own isVisible → pre-mutation ✓. And other nodes' isVisible never touches this node's bounds (frames' bounds only read for IFRAME frames — wait! `currentBounds.x += frameSnap.bounds.x` reads frame IFRAME's bounds. Could that frame's bounds have been mutated by the frame's own isVisible before this read? The frame IFRAME node's isVisible runs at END of its construct — AFTER all its descendants' constructs+isVisible (post-order). A descendant's isVisible reads the IFRAME's bounds DURING its own construct, which happens BEFORE the IFRAME's isVisible. So reads are pre-mutation ✓. But what about a node OUTSIDE the iframe whose frames list contains the IFRAME? Frames list only includes ancestors in the recursion path — constructed before the current node but their isVisible... The IFRAME is an ancestor of the current node; ancestor's isVisible runs after ALL descendants complete (post-order). Any node whose frames contain the IFRAME is a descendant of it. So all reads pre-mutation ✓. EXCEPT: memoized duplicates (same nodeId twice) — second construct returns memo early, no isVisible re-run ✓. And cross-iframe recursion builds a fresh lookup (fresh bounds objects) ✓.

